1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Human civilization has come a long way over the past two and a half thousand years.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:13,000
We live in a modern computer age, yet amazingly, the ancient Greeks created a type of computer,

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:19,000
precise mechanical clocks, and an accurate measuring device over two thousand years ago.

4
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:28,000
These devices have been hidden for thousands of years, but are now uncovered on modern Marvel's Ancient Discoveries One.

5
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:42,001
In ancient discoveries, we will travel back in time to discover the amazing ancient roots

6
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:50,440
of technologies we like to think of as modern.

7
00:00:50,441 --> 00:00:55,500
New research is beginning to suggest that many of the inventions of the last 200 years

8
00:00:55,501 --> 00:00:59,380
may in fact have already been known to the ancient Greeks and Romans.

9
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:06,320
Much of what we have recently discovered, we may in fact have just rediscovered.

10
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:11,320
The Greeks and Romans were also intensely practical people.

11
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:14,940
Many of the things which they devised, planned and invented

12
00:01:14,941 --> 00:01:19,260
preceded by 2,000 years, things that we like to think of as modern.

13
00:01:19,261 --> 00:01:29,400
At the heart of this story of ancient achievement lies a mysterious and arcane machine, one

14
00:01:29,401 --> 00:01:36,380
so complex that many have refused to believe it could have been built by the ancient Greeks.

15
00:01:36,381 --> 00:01:42,020
Hidden for centuries beneath the Mediterranean Sea, encased in thick corrosion, it remained

16
00:01:42,021 --> 00:01:45,660
an enigma until 20th century science glimpsed inside.

17
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:56,540
What they saw inside astonished them. A mass of gears and cogs. A machine.

18
00:01:56,541 --> 00:01:57,720
But what was

19
00:01:57,721 --> 00:02:03,380
it for? And who had created it?

20
00:02:03,381 --> 00:02:12,780
For a hundred years it has been a riddle which intrigued but baffled those who tried to understand

21
00:02:12,781 --> 00:02:16,660
the mysterious object known as the Antikythera mechanism.

22
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:22,760
But now, finally, its story can be told.

23
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:34,300
That story began just over 100 years ago, in the year 1900.

24
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:39,800
It was early spring when Captain Kondos and his crew of sponge divers

25
00:02:39,801 --> 00:02:45,020
found themselves sheltering from a storm, miles off course, by the little Greek island

26
00:02:45,021 --> 00:02:46,040
of Antikythera.

27
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:51,640
As the weather cleared, the captain decided to make the most of his unplanned stop by

28
00:02:51,641 --> 00:02:54,280
diving in the deep, clear waters off the island.

29
00:03:01,780 --> 00:03:07,800
The water was deep, some 200 feet, but then deep water was where the best sponges were

30
00:03:07,801 --> 00:03:08,501
found.

31
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:17,700
But so far down, there always is the danger a diver might return to the surface with the notorious bends.

32
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:23,280
Once in the water, the diver started his first descent.

33
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:30,280
But what awaited him on the bottom were not sponges.

34
00:03:31,420 --> 00:03:36,460
As he looked around, he saw what appeared to be dead bodies scattered in all directions.

35
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:53,800
Terrified, he quickly signaled to be pulled back to the boat.

36
00:03:55,640 --> 00:04:01,480
Some thought the diver was mad, perhaps he had the bends, but the truth was far, far stranger

37
00:04:01,481 --> 00:04:03,220
than that.

38
00:04:03,221 --> 00:04:10,820
Two thousand years ago, another ship had sailed these waters on its way from Rhodes to deliver

39
00:04:10,821 --> 00:04:24,821
a precious cargo to a wealthy Roman citizen.

40
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:29,940
Just like the sponge divers, this ship had also been caught in a storm.

41
00:04:29,941 --> 00:04:36,940
She too was driven far off course to the island of Antikythera.

42
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:46,440
But this ship was not destined to survive. Here she sunk. And here she still lay.

43
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:58,580
Captain Kondo sent another diver to investigate the wild story of the bodies.

44
00:04:59,940 --> 00:05:05,000
What had at first appeared to be dead bodies were in fact the most beautiful

45
00:05:05,001 --> 00:05:11,880
marble and bronze statues imaginable, the remnants of that ancient wreck.

46
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:20,220
Along with the statues other treasures such as decorated Greek vases and

47
00:05:20,221 --> 00:05:24,340
jewelry were winched to the surface.

48
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:38,940
It was the find of the decade, perhaps the century, and the breathtaking statues made

49
00:05:38,941 --> 00:05:41,440
front page news.

50
00:05:41,441 --> 00:05:47,000
One of the most perfect bronzes recovered was named the Antikythera Youth.

51
00:05:47,001 --> 00:05:52,380
The piece was universally acknowledged as a work of genius.

52
00:05:52,381 --> 00:06:05,380
But the greatest work of genius from the wreck still lay unrecognized.

53
00:06:05,381 --> 00:06:11,240
This rusting lump of corrosion held the key to one of the greatest periods in human history,

54
00:06:11,241 --> 00:06:17,380
the Hellenistic period, and perhaps the greatest achievement of that time.

55
00:06:17,381 --> 00:06:24,380
Two and a half thousand years ago, the Mediterranean was a place where science, philosophy and art flourished.

56
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:34,380
The Greeks, known as Hellenes, were living in what many regard as a golden age.

57
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:41,380
They had invented democracy, opened up the fields of mathematics and science, and introduced the world to philosophy.

58
00:06:41,381 --> 00:06:44,620
and science and introduced the world to philosophy.

59
00:06:46,960 --> 00:06:49,720
They were inquisitive and inventive,

60
00:06:50,080 --> 00:06:52,380
no different in many ways from us today.

61
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:57,980
In trying to understand their world,

62
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:00,340
they had created this strange machine.

63
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,380
All their 21st century descendants had to do

64
00:07:03,381 --> 00:07:05,900
was work out what they had created it for.

65
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:10,640
Part of that answer lay far from Antikythera

66
00:07:10,641 --> 00:07:12,860
in a London Museum display case.

67
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:18,240
Twenty years ago, Michael Wright,

68
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:21,220
curator of engineering at the Science Museum in London,

69
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:24,880
came upon another mysterious ancient mechanical device.

70
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:32,360
Although much less complex,

71
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:35,320
there were similarities with the Antikythera mechanism.

72
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:40,020
We call this the Byzantine sundial calendar.

73
00:07:40,640 --> 00:07:43,360
We think it dates from about 500 A.D.

74
00:07:44,560 --> 00:07:48,520
It was a mechanical calendar which shows the passing of days

75
00:07:48,521 --> 00:07:51,260
indicated by the age of the moon within the month.

76
00:07:52,380 --> 00:07:54,660
The gear wheels look surprisingly modern.

77
00:07:55,940 --> 00:07:59,320
Complicated mathematics and a detailed knowledge of astronomy

78
00:07:59,321 --> 00:08:02,060
would have been necessary to create such an object.

79
00:08:04,100 --> 00:08:06,960
Michael Wright decided to build a model of the device

80
00:08:06,961 --> 00:08:08,860
to explore how it would have worked.

81
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:17,640
The model shows the position of the Sun and Moon within the zodiac at any given date, as well as the age of the Moon.

82
00:08:17,641 --> 00:08:22,640
This instrument is much more simple than the Antikythera mechanism,

83
00:08:22,641 --> 00:08:28,640
but what's exciting about it is to have found another instrument,

84
00:08:28,641 --> 00:08:37,640
very obviously in the Hellenistic tradition, because of the Greek lettering, and also having gear wheels.

85
00:08:37,641 --> 00:08:38,587
gear wheels.

86
00:08:38,588 --> 00:08:44,980
So now we have not just one mechanism that tells us the Greeks had gears, we have

87
00:08:44,981 --> 00:08:46,900
two. We've doubled the evidence.

88
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:54,580
Although similar, it is estimated that the Sundial calendar only had eight gear wheels,

89
00:08:54,920 --> 00:09:01,160
where the Antikythera device had at least 29 and perhaps up to 70, allowing for much

90
00:09:01,161 --> 00:09:06,760
more complicated calculations. Could the Antikythera mechanism also be a mechanical

91
00:09:06,761 --> 00:09:12,680
calendar, one many times more complicated than the Byzantine machine, yet over 600 years

92
00:09:12,681 --> 00:09:13,381
older?

93
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:18,560
Who in the ancient world had the ability to design such a complex device?

94
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:24,880
The question has baffled experts since its discovery, but there is one legendary figure

95
00:09:24,881 --> 00:09:26,760
who could have been its creator.

96
00:09:29,220 --> 00:09:34,120
The remains of the Antikythera mechanism are on display at the National Archaeological

97
00:09:34,121 --> 00:09:39,140
Museum in Athens, Greece, but its original shape or dimension still cannot be determined.

98
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:41,740
Modern marvels will continue in a moment.

99
00:09:52,540 --> 00:09:57,660
The inventor of the Antikythera mechanism would have to have been an expert in both mathematics

100
00:09:57,661 --> 00:09:58,900
and engineering.

101
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:00,240
A genius.

102
00:10:00,840 --> 00:10:03,660
Centuries, perhaps even millennia, ahead of his time.

103
00:10:04,120 --> 00:10:11,120
One man who fit that description was Archimedes.

104
00:10:11,121 --> 00:10:19,120
Archimedes lived during the Hellenistic period in the Greek city-state of Syracuse,

105
00:10:19,121 --> 00:10:24,120
on what is today Sicily, off the coast of Italy.

106
00:10:24,121 --> 00:10:34,580
During his lifetime, the Mediterranean was a turbulent war-torn place.

107
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:43,820
A world in which his talent for military invention was put to good use by both his fellow Greeks

108
00:10:43,821 --> 00:10:49,420
and the aggressive new power of Rome that was slowly spreading its tentacles across the Mediterranean.

109
00:10:49,421 --> 00:10:56,420
Today he is perhaps best remembered for the revolutionary invention known as the Archimedean

110
00:10:56,700 --> 00:11:00,117
screw used to move water or seeds uphill.

111
00:11:00,118 --> 00:11:03,700
But Archimedes was also fascinated by other

112
00:11:03,701 --> 00:11:15,980
But Archimedes was also fascinated by other difficult mechanical problems, such as how

113
00:11:15,981 --> 00:11:23,580
to accurately measure distances.

114
00:11:23,581 --> 00:11:27,940
The greatest challenge of all, however, was to solve the puzzle of what caused the rising

115
00:11:27,941 --> 00:11:33,160
and setting of the sun, the changing seasons, and the strange movements of the moon and

116
00:11:33,161 --> 00:11:40,160
planets. What, in short, were the mechanics of time?

117
00:11:42,400 --> 00:11:47,060
Many years earlier, Archimedes had traveled across the Mediterranean to the most important

118
00:11:47,061 --> 00:11:51,460
cultural center of the Hellenistic world.

119
00:11:51,461 --> 00:11:56,280
Alexandria, Egypt.

120
00:11:56,281 --> 00:11:59,680
Alexandria was the most sophisticated place on Earth.

121
00:11:59,681 --> 00:12:04,500
Founded by Alexander the Great, it was now ruled by the descendants of one of his generals,

122
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:07,760
Greek soldiers who had risen to become pharaohs.

123
00:12:08,400 --> 00:12:13,680
It was a unique place, the meeting point of the age-old civilizations of the Mediterranean,

124
00:12:14,380 --> 00:12:18,080
a city of Egyptians, Greeks, Romans, and Persians.

125
00:12:20,220 --> 00:12:24,880
Here they combined the wisdom of the pharaohs with the brilliance of Greek philosophy

126
00:12:24,881 --> 00:12:28,620
to create the intellectual powerhouse of the ancient world.

127
00:12:29,680 --> 00:12:39,900
And it was here that the young Archimedes would be inspired by the work of a mechanical

128
00:12:39,901 --> 00:12:44,660
genius called Tesebius.

129
00:12:44,661 --> 00:12:58,661
He would have a simple but brilliant idea that would literally change time forever.

130
00:12:59,600 --> 00:13:04,060
was fortunate to be born in Alexandria.

131
00:13:04,061 --> 00:13:07,260
The place where all the knowledge of the ancient classical world

132
00:13:07,261 --> 00:13:12,740
was held in one vast library.

133
00:13:12,741 --> 00:13:15,960
A place where the great thinkers of antiquity studied,

134
00:13:15,961 --> 00:13:19,500
and where shelf upon shelf held the thousands of scrolls

135
00:13:19,501 --> 00:13:20,840
recording their work.

136
00:13:24,880 --> 00:13:27,540
The library was such a famous institution

137
00:13:27,541 --> 00:13:34,540
that people came here from all over the world to learn and invent.

138
00:13:43,820 --> 00:13:50,820
It was here that Tecebius' revolutionary invention was recorded,

139
00:13:50,821 --> 00:13:57,820
and where the young Archimedes may first have read of his work.

140
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:07,620
Tesebius is a figure who is often forgotten in history,

141
00:14:07,621 --> 00:14:10,980
yet his work paved the way for a technical revolution,

142
00:14:10,981 --> 00:14:17,980
the measurement of time.

143
00:14:20,820 --> 00:14:27,660
Yet, Tecebius came from humble beginnings.

144
00:14:27,661 --> 00:14:33,740
He was the son of a simple barber.

145
00:14:33,741 --> 00:14:38,680
As a young man, he had worked in his father's barber shop in Alexandria, inventing simple

146
00:14:38,681 --> 00:14:43,340
gadgets such as adjustable mirrors.

147
00:14:43,341 --> 00:14:47,560
And here he was surrounded by the constant dripping of water, which would inspire his

148
00:14:47,561 --> 00:14:50,560
great invention.

149
00:14:50,561 --> 00:14:57,560
Decebius knew that for thousands of years, the Egyptian had used ordinary water timers

150
00:14:58,240 --> 00:15:05,240
to mark the hours of the day. The famous Karnak water clock is one example.

151
00:15:07,900 --> 00:15:12,440
It is a vessel which was filled with water, and as it drained out through a spout at the

152
00:15:12,441 --> 00:15:15,983
bottom, the time could be read as the level dropped.

153
00:15:15,984 --> 00:15:18,300
Markings inside showed the passing

154
00:15:18,301 --> 00:15:23,300
and allowed the ancient Egyptians to measure the passing of time during the day or night.

155
00:15:23,301 --> 00:15:30,300
But this was still only a timer, known in Greek times as a klepsidra, not a constant clock.

156
00:15:30,301 --> 00:15:34,300
As the water level dropped, the water came out more slowly.

157
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:40,300
Tecebius, however, saw the solution.

158
00:15:40,301 --> 00:15:43,300
He realized that if the vessel was always full,

159
00:15:43,301 --> 00:15:46,300
then the water pressure outflow would always be the same.

160
00:15:46,301 --> 00:15:52,300
If he could master that, he knew he could create an accurate device which would change the world.

161
00:15:52,301 --> 00:15:56,300
This then was the challenge he decided to set himself.

162
00:15:56,301 --> 00:16:03,300
Persebius saw that the way to simplify the Egyptian Clepsydra

163
00:16:03,301 --> 00:16:11,300
was not to utilize outflow of water, but to try and obtain a uniform inflow of water.

164
00:16:11,301 --> 00:16:12,001
of water.

165
00:16:12,002 --> 00:16:17,180
The challenge was to keep the reservoir of the Klepsydra full at all times.

166
00:16:17,181 --> 00:16:21,020
And this is how Tesebius did it.

167
00:16:21,021 --> 00:16:28,220
First, he added another water tank above the main reservoir.

168
00:16:28,221 --> 00:16:33,620
This poured water into the top faster than it could flow out, meaning the reservoir was

169
00:16:33,621 --> 00:16:40,620
always full and any excess water could just run off into an overflow container.

170
00:16:46,080 --> 00:16:50,409
The water would always come out of the reservoir at the same speed.

171
00:16:50,410 --> 00:16:51,960
Now Tecebius just had to

172
00:16:51,961 --> 00:16:52,845
measure it.

173
00:16:52,846 --> 00:16:58,960
To do this, he decided to put another water tank under the constant outflow.

174
00:16:58,961 --> 00:17:00,401
the constant outflow.

175
00:17:00,402 --> 00:17:05,960
In this container he placed a float with a pointer on top and a scale next to it.

176
00:17:07,960 --> 00:17:12,352
When the level of the water rose, the pointer rose at a constant speed.

177
00:17:12,353 --> 00:17:13,960
It was a stroke of genius.

178
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:20,960
Tecebius had created the world's first mechanical clock, thanks to the dripping water in a barber shop.

179
00:17:28,960 --> 00:17:40,000
He had harnessed the power of water, and in the process, he had become the master of time.

180
00:17:42,780 --> 00:17:46,060
But measuring hours and minutes was only the start.

181
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:48,820
What else could this unique water clock do?

182
00:17:51,960 --> 00:17:56,000
The answer to that was kept here in the great library of Alexandria.

183
00:17:56,001 --> 00:18:02,000
Anyone in the ancient world wanting to understand time could come here and read Tecebius' books,

184
00:18:02,001 --> 00:18:09,000
which described the wonderful machines he was now building and the whole new subject he had invented, hydraulics.

185
00:18:09,001 --> 00:18:17,000
Before long, his clocks were not just dripping taps, but ornate machines decorated with gilded figures of gods and animals.

186
00:18:17,001 --> 00:18:20,000
And their mechanics were yet more elaborate, too.

187
00:18:20,001 --> 00:18:27,000
He devised a complex scale for the hours, shown here in white for the day and blue for the night.

188
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:36,000
The increasingly complicated series of gear wheels also allowed the scale to rotate very slowly

189
00:18:36,001 --> 00:18:39,000
to indicate the days within each month of the year.

190
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:44,000
This clock was also now a calendar.

191
00:19:00,026 --> 00:19:04,080
calendar, an automatic day and night, month and year, cuckoo clock.

192
00:19:04,081 --> 00:19:08,220
Archimedes was clearly fascinated by Tecebius' creations.

193
00:19:08,221 --> 00:19:15,220
He studied his clocks and used his own genius for invention to continue the work.

194
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:24,160
In an Arabic translation of Archimedes' work, dating back over a thousand years, we can

195
00:19:24,161 --> 00:19:31,160
see a tantalizing glimpse of his additions to Tesebius' water clock.

196
00:19:31,161 --> 00:19:41,560
This modern reconstruction shows Archimedes' elaborations to the Tesebius clock.

197
00:19:41,561 --> 00:19:47,340
He added a bird who dropped a small stone onto a bell, making a clock chime on the hour.

198
00:19:48,200 --> 00:19:51,040
The woman is a gorgon, with snakes for hair.

199
00:19:51,480 --> 00:19:56,340
When the bell chimes, you look up to see her eyes change color, indicating the time.

200
00:20:01,160 --> 00:20:08,160
Alexandria today is a busy modern port.

201
00:20:10,700 --> 00:20:17,700
A new building now stands in place of the famous library.

202
00:20:18,360 --> 00:20:21,620
Tragically the original library was burned to the ground

203
00:20:21,621 --> 00:20:25,200
and with it was lost nearly all the knowledge of the ancient world.

204
00:20:25,201 --> 00:20:32,200
Only a very few ancient texts remain which mention Tecebius' work.

205
00:20:32,201 --> 00:20:38,200
We may never know what else he invented, as not one single page of his own work survives.

206
00:20:47,200 --> 00:20:52,200
So much information has been lost that Tecebius has been largely forgotten.

207
00:20:55,200 --> 00:21:02,200
However, there is one legacy of his work, one clue which is still standing.

208
00:21:02,201 --> 00:21:09,200
Could this small Greek tower hold the key to an ancient secret?

209
00:21:09,201 --> 00:21:15,200
The dive, in which the Antikythera mechanism was discovered,

210
00:21:15,201 --> 00:21:20,200
happened more than 40 years before modern-day scuba gear was invented.

211
00:21:20,201 --> 00:21:26,200
The Kithra mechanism was discovered happened more than 40 years before modern-day scuba gear was invented.

212
00:21:26,201 --> 00:21:29,200
Modern marvels will continue in a moment.

213
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:45,200
This unassuming tower, tucked away in a corner of Athens, is one of the best-preserved buildings from antiquity.

214
00:21:45,201 --> 00:21:48,340
Its survival, almost untouched, is a miracle,

215
00:21:48,680 --> 00:21:54,720
and one due to the fact that for centuries it was believed to be the tomb of the philosophers Socrates and Plato.

216
00:21:55,700 --> 00:21:57,260
But this isn't a tomb.

217
00:21:57,800 --> 00:22:01,180
Carved on its eight faces are a clue to its real use.

218
00:22:01,800 --> 00:22:07,720
On each side, a worn sculpture of each of the eight winds can still be seen, along with a sundial.

219
00:22:15,200 --> 00:22:22,200
It was actually built by an astronomer around 200 years after the death of Tesebius, but

220
00:22:23,700 --> 00:22:25,438
is a monument to his genius.

221
00:22:25,439 --> 00:22:29,100
This building, now known as the Tower of the Winds, was the

222
00:22:29,101 --> 00:22:31,259
public clock of ancient Athens.

223
00:22:31,260 --> 00:22:35,020
Inside this tower, there once stood a huge and complex

224
00:22:35,021 --> 00:22:38,020
water clock, based on a Tesebius design.

225
00:22:38,021 --> 00:22:50,200
This clock was fed by a constant stream of water which ran from a spring on the Acropolis,

226
00:22:50,201 --> 00:22:54,280
from which the whole population of Athens could tell the time.

227
00:22:57,280 --> 00:23:00,540
But this was more than just a clock or even a calendar.

228
00:23:01,040 --> 00:23:06,440
Some believe this strange building housed a device that even charted the movements of the sun and moon

229
00:23:06,441 --> 00:23:08,960
in relation to the constellations of the zodiac.

230
00:23:10,280 --> 00:23:14,040
We know the Greeks were measuring hours, days, and months

231
00:23:14,041 --> 00:23:15,780
during the time of Tesebius.

232
00:23:16,120 --> 00:23:19,920
Could it be that they had also started to look above and measure the heavens?

233
00:23:24,480 --> 00:23:27,900
One man believed the answer may lie in the cogs and wheels

234
00:23:27,901 --> 00:23:29,540
of the Antikythera mechanism.

235
00:23:36,440 --> 00:23:50,160
In 1951, an English physicist Derek de Sola Price decided to find out for himself and

236
00:23:50,161 --> 00:23:53,880
examine the mechanism in detail for the first time.

237
00:23:53,881 --> 00:24:01,780
De Sola Price traveled to Athens to look at the mechanism.

238
00:24:01,781 --> 00:24:06,940
The pieces had lain largely untouched since its discovery fifty years earlier.

239
00:24:06,941 --> 00:24:13,220
The device had disintegrated further, exposing pieces of the gears which he was able to study.

240
00:24:17,180 --> 00:24:22,180
He became determined to crack the secrets of the Antikythera mechanism.

241
00:24:22,181 --> 00:24:35,260
De Sola Price spent much of his time at the National Museum of Greece in Athens, probing

242
00:24:35,261 --> 00:24:43,380
the secrets of this ancient enigma.

243
00:24:43,381 --> 00:24:52,160
Something told him that these fragments were the real treasure from the Antikythera wreck.

244
00:24:52,161 --> 00:24:58,760
Using new developments in X-ray technology, he could now see what its discoverers 50 years

245
00:24:58,761 --> 00:25:00,760
before could not.

246
00:25:00,761 --> 00:25:03,540
Looking through the corrosion, he was amazed.

247
00:25:03,541 --> 00:25:07,600
A machine so complex, it could almost be modern.

248
00:25:07,601 --> 00:25:11,600
He had to know what it was for.

249
00:25:11,601 --> 00:25:17,600
His meticulous studies of the cogs, gear ratios, and inscriptions gave him clues to the possible

250
00:25:17,601 --> 00:25:18,880
purpose of the mechanism.

251
00:25:22,160 --> 00:25:31,160
Remnants of a wooden box which held the device also had Greek writing, which gave further tantalizing hints.

252
00:25:32,160 --> 00:25:41,160
Using this information, de Sola Price developed a theory and put together a model of how he believed the Antikythera mechanism could have worked.

253
00:25:41,161 --> 00:25:53,160
He realized the mechanism was an extremely sophisticated device for calculating the relative movements of the sun and moon.

254
00:25:53,161 --> 00:25:57,160
It also seemed to show the days of the month as lunar phases.

255
00:26:11,160 --> 00:26:18,160
De Sola Price had established its mechanical complexity and knew that the knowledge required

256
00:26:18,161 --> 00:26:22,400
to create such a machine was immense.

257
00:26:22,401 --> 00:26:27,280
He believed that at the front of the mechanism a bronze dial showed the date and positions

258
00:26:27,281 --> 00:26:29,080
of the sun and moon.

259
00:26:29,081 --> 00:26:34,760
A dial at the back would indicate the month, possibly within a 12-month year.

260
00:26:34,761 --> 00:26:40,480
Another dial at the back seemed to show either a cycle of 47 months or 4 years.

261
00:26:41,160 --> 00:26:48,160
called the mechanism a calendar computer.

262
00:27:01,720 --> 00:27:06,920
For anyone in the ancient world, such a device would have been invaluable.

263
00:27:06,921 --> 00:27:10,580
To understand the movement of the sun and moon within the heavens

264
00:27:10,581 --> 00:27:13,540
was to look into the minds of the gods.

265
00:27:13,541 --> 00:27:15,980
Many believed then, as some do today,

266
00:27:15,981 --> 00:27:18,680
that the positions of the sun, moon, and stars

267
00:27:18,681 --> 00:27:22,940
at the time of someone's birth may influence their later life.

268
00:27:22,941 --> 00:27:26,040
What complex horoscope software does today,

269
00:27:26,041 --> 00:27:28,340
the Antikythera mechanism may have done

270
00:27:28,341 --> 00:27:30,040
over two millennia earlier.

271
00:27:34,560 --> 00:27:36,760
To the priests and astrologers of the day,

272
00:27:36,761 --> 00:27:40,500
This extraordinary machine could have been a window on the gods.

273
00:27:41,400 --> 00:27:43,580
But where could such a device have come from?

274
00:27:44,200 --> 00:27:46,500
De Sola Price had an extraordinary theory.

275
00:27:47,380 --> 00:27:51,760
What if machines like the Antikythera Mechanism were part of municipal clocks,

276
00:27:52,020 --> 00:27:54,920
powered by the very water clocks that Decebius had invented?

277
00:27:55,540 --> 00:27:58,420
What if they were commonplace in the Hellenistic world?

278
00:27:58,880 --> 00:28:01,400
Was this the secret of the Tower of the Winds?

279
00:28:01,401 --> 00:28:06,920
not just a sundial or even a clock, but an automatic model of the movements of

280
00:28:06,921 --> 00:28:12,400
the Sun and Moon within the universe. De Sola Price also suggested that the

281
00:28:12,401 --> 00:28:17,400
Antikythera mechanism was a later copy of one of Archimedes machines lost to the

282
00:28:17,401 --> 00:28:26,400
world since the destruction of the Great Library at Alexandria.

283
00:28:26,401 --> 00:28:31,180
This was an intriguing theory which could lead others to try to reveal the

284
00:28:31,181 --> 00:28:33,840
truth about the Antikythera mechanism.

285
00:28:39,520 --> 00:28:43,220
In 1953, Jacques Cousteau and the crew of the Calypso

286
00:28:43,221 --> 00:28:45,900
explored the Antikythera wreck site.

287
00:28:45,901 --> 00:28:49,260
When they returned in 1973, they found previously

288
00:28:49,261 --> 00:28:51,200
undiscovered gold bars.

289
00:28:51,201 --> 00:28:53,260
Modern marvels will continue in a moment.

290
00:29:01,180 --> 00:29:07,180
The Antikythera mechanism mystery continued.

291
00:29:07,181 --> 00:29:13,180
One man was to read de Sola Price's work and was to spend the next 30 years pondering his theory.

292
00:29:15,180 --> 00:29:19,180
When Michael Wright from the Science Museum first read the paper,

293
00:29:19,181 --> 00:29:24,180
he was immediately fascinated by the notion that the ancient Greeks had complex engineering.

294
00:29:24,181 --> 00:29:30,180
Wright decided to go to Athens himself to examine and re-X-ray the mechanism.

295
00:29:31,180 --> 00:29:45,180
We've got this view with several of the pieces put together, and it helps me to build up a more complete picture of just how the gear wheels were arranged inside the

296
00:29:45,401 --> 00:29:47,900
box before it all fell apart.

297
00:29:47,901 --> 00:29:57,900
Although intrigued by de Sola Price's theory of the Antikythera mechanism,

298
00:29:58,580 --> 00:30:02,300
Wright believed there was more to the device than de Sola Price had realized.

299
00:30:03,420 --> 00:30:06,940
I went back and read Gears from the Greeks again

300
00:30:06,941 --> 00:30:11,060
and started to find problems with Price's account.

301
00:30:11,480 --> 00:30:14,300
That's when I resolved I really had to look at the thing for myself.

302
00:30:14,301 --> 00:30:19,300
But for Michael Wright to understand this strange device,

303
00:30:19,301 --> 00:30:22,300
he knew he needed to do more than just look at it.

304
00:30:22,301 --> 00:30:25,300
He needed to try to build one for himself.

305
00:30:27,300 --> 00:30:30,300
After de Sola Price's publication,

306
00:30:30,301 --> 00:30:34,300
many academics simply refused to admit that the mechanism could be so old.

307
00:30:34,301 --> 00:30:37,300
The complex gearing seemed too modern.

308
00:30:40,300 --> 00:30:42,300
How could ancient peoples have calculated

309
00:30:42,301 --> 00:30:46,240
and precisely cut the fine teeth on each wheel, they asked.

310
00:30:50,620 --> 00:30:56,180
Surely this was either a fake or a later machine that just happened to be lost on the site of the ancient wreck.

311
00:30:57,060 --> 00:31:00,240
So Wright set to work, determined to prove them wrong.

312
00:31:12,300 --> 00:31:20,860
Using only tools similar to those available in the ancient world,

313
00:31:21,320 --> 00:31:23,020
with the Greeks' knowledge of geometry,

314
00:31:23,021 --> 00:31:26,540
he set out to copy these elaborate gears in his workshop.

315
00:31:27,500 --> 00:31:30,280
Measuring and cutting gears with only a pair of compasses,

316
00:31:30,460 --> 00:31:34,340
a metal file, and a good eye for detail was not going to be easy.

317
00:31:34,760 --> 00:31:36,220
But it was possible.

318
00:31:36,860 --> 00:31:40,080
After many hours of practice, Wright proved beyond doubt

319
00:31:40,081 --> 00:31:42,920
that with patience and skill it could be done.

320
00:31:47,980 --> 00:31:52,600
You can't deny the evidence that the Antikythera mechanism exists.

321
00:31:53,100 --> 00:31:56,600
When you look at it closely, it's very accomplished work,

322
00:31:56,800 --> 00:31:58,600
both in design and in execution.

323
00:31:59,440 --> 00:32:01,440
Why shouldn't the ancient Greeks have done it?

324
00:32:02,520 --> 00:32:05,120
We know from translations of his work

325
00:32:05,121 --> 00:32:08,340
that Archimedes was familiar with these types of cogs,

326
00:32:08,341 --> 00:32:12,940
And now it was the study of these gears that was to help solve yet another mystery,

327
00:32:13,240 --> 00:32:17,520
involving one of his other mechanical marvels, the mystery of the odometer.

328
00:32:24,220 --> 00:32:27,600
We all use odometers every time we get in a car.

329
00:32:28,220 --> 00:32:30,760
It's the device which measures the distance we've traveled.

330
00:32:30,761 --> 00:32:40,940
But remarkably, this device was in use long, long before cars and was crucial to the development

331
00:32:40,941 --> 00:32:44,600
of the Roman Empire.

332
00:32:44,601 --> 00:32:52,540
The odometer was said to have been invented by Archimedes while he was working for the

333
00:32:52,541 --> 00:32:53,540
Roman army.

334
00:32:53,541 --> 00:32:58,700
It had been decreed that every road should be marked each mile with a milestone, but

335
00:32:58,701 --> 00:33:05,700
Roman roads stretched hundreds of miles, so how could they be accurately measured?

336
00:33:10,580 --> 00:33:15,420
By creating a cart that would accurately calculate distance, Archimedes solved the

337
00:33:15,421 --> 00:33:22,420
problem and the milestones could be laid out.

338
00:33:23,940 --> 00:33:28,540
This accurate measurement of distance would prove crucial in planning troop movements,

339
00:33:28,541 --> 00:33:33,040
and that gave Rome the edge on her enemies.

340
00:33:33,041 --> 00:33:38,880
Archimedes had set the Romans on the path to empire

341
00:33:38,881 --> 00:33:47,040
and started a train of events that would ironically lead to his own death.

342
00:33:47,041 --> 00:33:50,760
Today the most famous of Roman roads, the Appian Way,

343
00:33:50,761 --> 00:33:57,760
is still marked in places by the milestones that Archimedes' machine laid out.

344
00:33:58,540 --> 00:34:04,400
But there is a problem with this apparently simple story,

345
00:34:04,960 --> 00:34:08,540
one that Professor André Sleeswick from Groningen in Holland

346
00:34:08,541 --> 00:34:13,800
would eventually solve with the aid of the ghostly X-ray images of the Antikythera mechanism.

347
00:34:13,801 --> 00:34:25,620
them.

348
00:34:25,621 --> 00:34:31,360
As no originals survive, the only clues to how the odometer worked are a few faded drawings

349
00:34:31,361 --> 00:34:38,360
in a Renaissance book based on a description of an odometer by a Roman author called Vitruvius.

350
00:34:38,361 --> 00:34:40,212
The drawings look fine on paper.

351
00:34:40,213 --> 00:34:43,280
The problem is when you try to build them, they don't

352
00:34:43,281 --> 00:34:49,842
work. Even Leonardo da Vinci attempted it.

353
00:34:49,843 --> 00:34:56,560
These are his sketches of an odometer which

354
00:34:56,561 --> 00:34:58,840
he attempted but could not successfully build.

355
00:35:13,280 --> 00:35:23,200
Professor Sleeswick started to make simple models to see if he could crack the odometer puzzle.

356
00:35:24,060 --> 00:35:29,440
This was then developed into a quarter-size model, and this was a model with a difference.

357
00:35:31,620 --> 00:35:36,200
Where even Leonardo had failed, Sleeswick's model worked.

358
00:35:36,201 --> 00:35:43,520
The mechanics are simple.

359
00:35:43,600 --> 00:35:46,360
As the wheels turn when the vehicle travels,

360
00:35:47,160 --> 00:35:51,660
a gear with a single tooth pushes around a huge gear with 400 teeth.

361
00:35:52,200 --> 00:35:54,440
Every time the large wheel turns once,

362
00:35:54,880 --> 00:35:58,940
a small stone would be automatically dropped into a box below the device.

363
00:35:59,580 --> 00:36:01,480
This allowed miles to be marked.

364
00:36:06,200 --> 00:36:20,200
After the first Punic War, by decision of the Senate of Rome, milestones had to be placed

365
00:36:23,840 --> 00:36:25,880
on all major roads.

366
00:36:25,881 --> 00:36:32,620
And that also gives us an idea of who the inventor might have been, in this case, Archimedes.

367
00:36:32,621 --> 00:36:35,040
So what was Sleeswick's secret?

368
00:36:35,041 --> 00:36:38,440
The answer was in the shape of the teeth on the gears.

369
00:36:38,441 --> 00:36:42,940
While da Vinci and others had assumed they were square, Sleeswick had actually seen the

370
00:36:42,941 --> 00:36:46,900
pointed teeth on x-rays of the Antikythera mechanism.

371
00:36:46,901 --> 00:36:54,720
Changing the shape of those teeth made all the difference.

372
00:36:54,721 --> 00:37:00,220
Archimedes' odometer, for so long thought of as just an impossible dream, was back and

373
00:37:00,221 --> 00:37:07,220
and working just as the great man had intended.

374
00:37:11,060 --> 00:37:14,460
All thanks to that most mysterious of ancient objects,

375
00:37:14,461 --> 00:37:19,040
the Antikythera mechanism.

376
00:37:19,041 --> 00:37:26,040
A device whose true secrets were yet to come to light.

377
00:37:26,041 --> 00:37:30,640
In May 1983, an Italian stamp was issued to honor Archimedes.

378
00:37:31,280 --> 00:37:37,380
However, the image printed was actually that of Archidamus III, a king of Sparta from 3rd century BC.

379
00:37:38,280 --> 00:37:40,220
Modern marbles will continue in a moment.

380
00:37:40,221 --> 00:37:54,221
Back in London, Michael Wright was building his working Antikythera mechanism model using ancient techniques.

381
00:37:59,320 --> 00:38:08,140
Far more complex than the odometer, but using similar gears, this would be the most complete model of the Antikythera mechanism ever built.

382
00:38:10,220 --> 00:38:18,020
As each gear slotted into place, it soon became clear de Sola Price may have got the details wrong, but his theory was right.

383
00:38:18,800 --> 00:38:25,660
The Antikythera mechanism was an automated calculating device, perhaps even a type of computer.

384
00:38:27,360 --> 00:38:39,520
You could call this thing a computer in the sense that you put in some data and it gives you out related data.

385
00:38:40,220 --> 00:38:45,220
An ancient computer is a difficult concept to imagine,

386
00:38:45,221 --> 00:38:49,220
yet in many ways that is exactly what the Antikythera mechanism is.

387
00:38:53,220 --> 00:38:58,220
It is astounding to compare it with the machine designed by Charles Babbage in the 1830s,

388
00:38:58,221 --> 00:39:01,220
which is often cited as the world's first computer,

389
00:39:01,221 --> 00:39:05,220
and the machine which led to our modern high-tech age.

390
00:39:05,221 --> 00:39:11,220
This was the first modern device which could make automatic calculations.

391
00:39:11,221 --> 00:39:15,220
The technology is startlingly similar to the Antikythera mechanism,

392
00:39:15,221 --> 00:39:19,220
yet there is 2,000 years difference between the two.

393
00:39:22,220 --> 00:39:28,220
Wright believes that the Antikythera mechanism was more complex than the solar price imagined,

394
00:39:28,221 --> 00:39:33,220
and most importantly also showed the changing positions of the planets,

395
00:39:33,221 --> 00:39:36,820
as well as the Sun and Moon over time.

396
00:39:36,821 --> 00:39:38,720
In Wright's model of the mechanism,

397
00:39:38,721 --> 00:39:42,120
a knob is turned to set the days and months.

398
00:39:42,121 --> 00:39:45,020
Intricate gearing then moves a series of indicators

399
00:39:45,021 --> 00:39:48,420
which represent the Sun, Moon and planets.

400
00:39:48,421 --> 00:39:50,120
The knob could be turned every day

401
00:39:50,121 --> 00:39:52,620
so the position of the planets could be monitored

402
00:39:52,621 --> 00:39:56,120
throughout the year, or it could be set to a specific date

403
00:39:56,121 --> 00:39:58,720
to show their positions at that time.

404
00:39:58,721 --> 00:40:03,020
It is, in effect, a mechanical model of the Greek universe.

405
00:40:03,021 --> 00:40:09,760
Today, astrologers need a complex computer program to carry out exactly the function of the Antikythera mechanism.

406
00:40:18,040 --> 00:40:21,760
So could this machine have been created by Archimedes himself?

407
00:40:22,480 --> 00:40:28,300
We know from ancient texts that he built planetaria, but could he have designed this one?

408
00:40:28,301 --> 00:40:35,300
It's difficult to make a direct connection between the Antikythera mechanism and Archimedes.

409
00:40:35,301 --> 00:40:42,300
What we can say with certainty was the revolution that Archimedes pioneered in mathematics and geometry

410
00:40:42,301 --> 00:40:47,300
was necessary for those who then invented the Antikythera mechanism.

411
00:40:47,301 --> 00:40:54,300
Without Archimedes and his advances, it's difficult to think that the Antikythera mechanism could have been invented at all.

412
00:40:58,300 --> 00:41:05,060
Archimedes' own life was cut short before he could finish his revolutionary work on

413
00:41:05,061 --> 00:41:08,340
mathematics and mechanics.

414
00:41:08,341 --> 00:41:12,540
During the Roman sacking of his home city of Syracuse, it is said a Roman soldier was

415
00:41:12,541 --> 00:41:15,740
sent to find him and bring him back alive.

416
00:41:15,741 --> 00:41:27,940
When Archimedes refused to leave his work, the soldier became angry and killed him.

417
00:41:27,941 --> 00:41:33,500
was an ironic end for a man who had done so much to make Rome such a formidable fighting force.

418
00:41:34,880 --> 00:41:40,620
His death marked the beginning of the end of the Hellenistic tradition of great invention and science.

419
00:41:43,760 --> 00:41:49,500
His work, however, was preserved, stored on the endless shelves of the great library of Alexandria,

420
00:41:50,360 --> 00:41:53,740
a treasure greater than any the Romans looted from Syracuse.

421
00:41:53,741 --> 00:42:03,340
but even that legacy was not to last the library itself fell victim to war and destruction

422
00:42:03,341 --> 00:42:09,560
being burned to the ground several times over the following 800 years eventually most of the

423
00:42:09,561 --> 00:42:15,280
collection including much of Archimedes work was lost or destroyed in the fires if only the

424
00:42:15,281 --> 00:42:23,120
library had survived we may have inherited the insights of such great thinkers without them we

425
00:42:23,121 --> 00:42:28,240
We have been forced to reinvent much that we are only now realizing was already known

426
00:42:28,241 --> 00:42:32,240
over two thousand years ago.

427
00:42:32,241 --> 00:42:45,000
If we had not lost this ancient wisdom, how much more advanced might science and technology

428
00:42:45,001 --> 00:42:45,880
be today?

429
00:42:53,120 --> 00:42:58,120
And what else may still lie hidden or lost from that time?

430
00:42:58,121 --> 00:43:02,120
Without that sudden storm all those centuries ago,

431
00:43:02,121 --> 00:43:04,120
that ancient ship might not have foundered,

432
00:43:04,121 --> 00:43:08,120
taking her cargo of statues, pottery, and that incredible machine

433
00:43:08,121 --> 00:43:10,120
to the bottom of the Mediterranean Sea.

434
00:43:10,121 --> 00:43:15,120
If a group of sponge divers had not stumbled upon the wreck 2,000 years later,

435
00:43:15,121 --> 00:43:20,120
the Antikythera mechanism might be lying there today, slowly corroding.

436
00:43:20,121 --> 00:43:27,120
And what other technological marvels from that time might still be awaiting discovery?

437
00:43:40,120 --> 00:43:48,120
I think that these mentions we have in the literature and this one instrument are just the tips of icebergs

438
00:43:48,121 --> 00:43:55,520
of icebergs showing that there was a great deal more to this tradition that we haven't heard about.

439
00:43:56,100 --> 00:44:00,700
2,000 years ago, there was a world more advanced than we had dared to believe,

440
00:44:01,260 --> 00:44:05,100
and more secrets from that time may still lie waiting to be discovered.

441
00:44:05,720 --> 00:44:09,480
Ancient discoveries we are only just beginning to understand.

442
00:44:11,880 --> 00:44:15,600
Next on the History Channel, a 2,000-year-old machine,

443
00:44:15,601 --> 00:44:23,400
So far ahead of its time that world-class researchers are staking their reputations on the claim that ancient Greeks invented the computer.

444
00:44:24,160 --> 00:44:25,077
And this is it.

445
00:44:25,078 --> 00:44:30,760
The Antikythera Mechanism and other amazing ancient discoveries, next on the History Channel.

